    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. another thing though—the shards are pretty badly broken up and scattered across this specific layer, it's making it hard to see any patterns. does the way they're distributed or the way they've fragmented say anything about how they were deposited, or is that just noise?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The distribution and fragmentation patterns can indeed offer clues about deposition, moving beyond just the ceramic type. High concentrations in small areas might suggest a discard midden or destruction layer, indicating rapid, localized dumping. Conversely, sparse, evenly scattered fragments could imply less localized activity or post-depositional disturbance, such as bioturbation or earth movement. Ultimately, these taphonomic observations, when combined with stratigraphy, help build a picture of how the assemblage ended up in the ground.",
      "source": "target",
